msgid "<0/> {left, plural, one {due} other {due}}"
msgstr "<0/> {left, plural, one {due} other {due}}"

msgid "<0/> <1/> Lesson {going} explains what the <2>-ma</2> form means after <3>lähen</3>. Tap a verb to hear it in a sentence."
msgstr "<0/> <1/> Lesson {going} explains what the <2>-ma</2> form means after <3>lähen</3>. Tap a verb to hear it in a sentence."

msgid "<0/> and <1/> both mean not, but they do different jobs. <2>Ei</2> says what someone doesn’t do: <3/>, I’m not sitting down. <4>Ära</4> tells someone not to do it: <5/> A command never starts with <6>ei</6>."
msgstr "<0/> and <1/> both mean not, but they do different jobs. <2>Ei</2> says what someone doesn’t do: <3/>, I’m not sitting down. <4>Ära</4> tells someone not to do it: <5/> A command never starts with <6>ei</6>."

msgid "<0/> and <1/> both translate as to read. Estonian uses each after different words: after <2/>, I want, comes the <3>-da</3> form, <4/>. Lesson {infinitives} shows which words take which."
msgstr "<0/> and <1/> both translate as to read. Estonian uses each after different words: after <2/>, I want, comes the <3>-da</3> form, <4/>. Lesson {infinitives} shows which words take which."

msgid "<0/> and <1/> change their long vowel: <2/>, <3/>. Learn these four whole."
msgstr "<0/> and <1/> change their long vowel: <2/>, <3/>. Learn these four whole."

msgid "<0/> asks about people and <1/> about things: <2/> Who is that? <3/> What is that?"
msgstr "<0/> asks about people and <1/> about things: <2/> Who is that? <3/> What is that?"

msgid "<0/> asks how many, and the noun after it takes its third form too. For a price, ask with <1>kui palju</1>, how much, from lesson {questions}."
msgstr "<0/> asks how many, and the noun after it takes its third form too. For a price, ask with <1>kui palju</1>, how much, from lesson {questions}."

msgid "<0/> at the start turns a sentence into a yes-or-no question. Lesson {questions} covers kas and the question words."
msgstr "<0/> at the start turns a sentence into a yes-or-no question. Lesson {questions} covers kas and the question words."

msgid "<0/> became <1/> before <2/>. That’s the genitive, the form lesson {genitive} taught for whose: <3>kasti</3>, the box’s. Each place word takes it: <4/> becomes <5>laua all</5>."
msgstr "<0/> became <1/> before <2/>. That’s the genitive, the form lesson {genitive} taught for whose: <3>kasti</3>, the box’s. Each place word takes it: <4/> becomes <5>laua all</5>."

msgid "<0/> becomes <1/>, and the verb takes its <2>-ge</2> or <3>-ke</3> form: <4/> <5/> Said politely to one person, the sentence to keep is <6/>"
msgstr "<0/> becomes <1/>, and the verb takes its <2>-ge</2> or <3>-ke</3> form: <4/> <5/> Said politely to one person, the sentence to keep is <6/>"

msgid "<0/> can also say that something might happen. This sentence can mean he or she is allowed to come tomorrow, or might: <1/>"
msgstr "<0/> can also say that something might happen. This sentence can mean he or she is allowed to come tomorrow, or might: <1/>"

msgid "<0/> has no ending, and it serves both he or she and they. That makes <1/> irregular: other verbs add an ending there too, as lesson {regularVerbs} shows."
msgstr "<0/> has no ending, and it serves both he or she and they. That makes <1/> irregular: other verbs add an ending there too, as lesson {regularVerbs} shows."

msgid "<0/> has the <1>-vad</1> of they from lesson {present}, and <2/> is the plural of lesson {plural}. The person never changes the verb: <3/> is they like the apple. Switch between One thing and More than one, and tap a line to hear it."
msgstr "<0/> has the <1>-vad</1> of they from lesson {present}, and <2/> is the plural of lesson {plural}. The person never changes the verb: <3/> is they like the apple. Switch between One thing and More than one, and tap a line to hear it."

msgid "<0/> I have a cat. To say you don’t, use <1/> from lesson {negation}, and the cat takes its third form: <2/> (Lesson {have} teaches <3>Mul on</3>.)"
msgstr "<0/> I have a cat. To say you don’t, use <1/> from lesson {negation}, and the cat takes its third form: <2/> (Lesson {have} teaches <3>Mul on</3>.)"

msgid "<0/> is a cup of coffee. The amount comes first, and what it measures follows in its third form: <1/> becomes <2/>."
msgstr "<0/> is a cup of coffee. The amount comes first, and what it measures follows in its third form: <1/> becomes <2/>."

msgid "<0/> is a hundred and <1/> a thousand, with no “one” in front of either. Hundreds join into one word, <2/>, but thousands stay separate: <3/>."
msgstr "<0/> is a hundred and <1/> a thousand, with no “one” in front of either. Hundreds join into one word, <2/>, but thousands stay separate: <3/>."

msgid "<0/> is a hundred; <1/> means send. A double letter is a longer sound, and it makes a different word. Consonants work the same way: <2/> is a hen, and <3/> tells one person to carry something."
msgstr "<0/> is a hundred; <1/> means send. A double letter is a longer sound, and it makes a different word. Consonants work the same way: <2/> is a hen, and <3/> tells one person to carry something."

msgid "<0/> is about being allowed. With <1>ei</1> it says mustn’t: <2/> In a question it asks whether you may, as <3/> does too: <4/> Tap one to hear its sentence."
msgstr "<0/> is about being allowed. With <1>ei</1> it says mustn’t: <2/> In a question it asks whether you may, as <3/> does too: <4/> Tap one to hear its sentence."

msgid "<0/> is at home. Now answer <1/> six ways: tap a place."
msgstr "<0/> is at home. Now answer <1/> six ways: tap a place."

msgid "<0/> is for what you learned. To say you know how to swim: <1/> <2/> says nothing stops you: <3/>"
msgstr "<0/> is for what you learned. To say you know how to swim: <1/> <2/> says nothing stops you: <3/>"

msgid "<0/> is he and she alike; Estonian pronouns have no gender. <1/> is also the polite you to one person, like French vous."
msgstr "<0/> is he and she alike; Estonian pronouns have no gender. <1/> is also the polite you to one person, like French vous."

msgid "<0/> is I have to, and it takes the <1>-ma</1> form from lesson {infinitives}: <2/>"
msgstr "<0/> is I have to, and it takes the <1>-ma</1> form from lesson {infinitives}: <2/>"

msgid "<0/> is I, the one who does something: <1/> <2/> is my, for what you own: <3/>"
msgstr "<0/> is I, the one who does something: <1/> <2/> is my, for what you own: <3/>"

msgid "<0/> is must, but <1>ei pea</1> is don’t have to: <2/> For mustn’t, Estonian switches to <3>ei tohi</3>: <4/> <5/>, no need, also leaves it to you. Tap one to hear it said about swimming."
msgstr "<0/> is must, but <1>ei pea</1> is don’t have to: <2/> For mustn’t, Estonian switches to <3>ei tohi</3>: <4/> <5/>, no need, also leaves it to you. Tap one to hear it said about swimming."

msgid "<0/> is one cat; <1/> is two cats. After a number from two up, a noun takes its third form, the partitive, and stays singular: <2/> has no plural ending."
msgstr "<0/> is one cat; <1/> is two cats. After a number from two up, a noun takes its third form, the partitive, and stays singular: <2/> has no plural ending."

msgid "<0/> is the <1>-l</1> form of <2/>. That ending says on or at in lesson {places}, as in <3/>. You have met one already: <4/> In it, <5/> is “at you”."
msgstr "<0/> is the <1>-l</1> form of <2/>. That ending says on or at in lesson {places}, as in <3/>. You have met one already: <4/> In it, <5/> is “at you”."

msgid "<0/> is the same for one and many, so with it only the noun changes: <1/> <2/> Switch between One and Many, and tap a sentence to hear it."
msgstr "<0/> is the same for one and many, so with it only the noun changes: <1/> <2/> Switch between One and Many, and tap a sentence to hear it."

msgid "<0/> is the town; <1/> is in Tallinn. Estonian says in with an ending, not a word in front: it puts <2>-s</2> on the end of the name. I live in Tallinn is <3/>"
msgstr "<0/> is the town; <1/> is in Tallinn. Estonian says in with an ending, not a word in front: it puts <2>-s</2> on the end of the name. I live in Tallinn is <3/>"

msgid "<0/> is tomorrow and <1/> yesterday. Put <2>üle-</2> in front to go one day further: <3/>, the day after tomorrow, and <4/>, the day before yesterday. Pick a day to be today."
msgstr "<0/> is tomorrow and <1/> yesterday. Put <2>üle-</2> in front to go one day further: <3/>, the day after tomorrow, and <4/>, the day before yesterday. Pick a day to be today."

msgid "<0/> is twelve and <1/> is twenty. Both start with kaks, so listen to how the word ends."
msgstr "<0/> is twelve and <1/> is twenty. Both start with kaks, so listen to how the word ends."

msgid "<0/> is with a friend. To say with, Estonian adds <1>-ga</1> to the genitive from lesson {genitive}: <2/>, <3/>, <4>sõbraga</4>."
msgstr "<0/> is with a friend. To say with, Estonian adds <1>-ga</1> to the genitive from lesson {genitive}: <2/>, <3/>, <4>sõbraga</4>."

msgid "<0/> is yes and <1/> is no. When the question asks with olema, to be, the usual no is <2/>: he or she isn’t, I’m not. When you don’t know, say <3/>."
msgstr "<0/> is yes and <1/> is no. When the question asks with olema, to be, the usual no is <2/>: he or she isn’t, I’m not. When you don’t know, say <3/>."

msgid "<0/> is your, short for <1/>; lesson {genitive} explains the form. <2/> swaps the -s of kus for -st, out of: where from."
msgstr "<0/> is your, short for <1/>; lesson {genitive} explains the form. <2/> swaps the -s of kus for -st, out of: where from."

msgid "<0/> means gladly. <1>Jah, hea meelega!</1> says yes to an invitation."
msgstr "<0/> means gladly. <1>Jah, hea meelega!</1> says yes to an invitation."

msgid "<0/> means half, and it looks ahead. <1/> is halfway to four, so it’s 3:30, not 4:30."
msgstr "<0/> means half, and it looks ahead. <1/> is halfway to four, so it’s 3:30, not 4:30."

msgid "<0/> means holy, so this is the holy day."
msgstr "<0/> means holy, so this is the holy day."

