msgstr "A free lesson on Estonian verbs in the present tense: the -ma form, the stem, and the six endings -n, -d, -b, -me, -te and -vad on ten everyday verbs. Every sentence is read aloud."

msgid "A free lesson on Estonian’s three “inside” cases, the inessive, illative and elative: kus, kuhu and kust, -s for in, -sse for into (Tartusse) and -st for out of (koolist), the short forms kooli, poodi and koju, and how to say where you’re from. Every sentence is read aloud."
msgstr "A free lesson on Estonian’s three “inside” cases, the inessive, illative and elative: kus, kuhu and kust, -s for in, -sse for into (Tartusse) and -st for out of (koolist), the short forms kooli, poodi and koju, and how to say where you’re from. Every sentence is read aloud."

msgid "A free lesson on Estonian’s three “on” cases, the adessive, allative and ablative: kus, kuhu and kust, -l for on and at (laual, tööl), -le for onto and to (lauale, tööle) and -lt for off and from (laualt, töölt), which places take -le and which -sse, and -le and -lt on a person (emale, emalt). Every sentence is read aloud."
msgstr "A free lesson on Estonian’s three “on” cases, the adessive, allative and ablative: kus, kuhu and kust, -l for on and at (laual, tööl), -le for onto and to (lauale, tööle) and -lt for off and from (laualt, töölt), which places take -le and which -sse, and -le and -lt on a person (emale, emalt). Every sentence is read aloud."

msgid "A free lesson on how to say let’s and let him or her in Estonian: the we form without me (teeme, vaatame, lähme) and las with the present (las ta tuleb, las nad mängivad, las ma aitan). Every sentence is read aloud."
msgstr "A free lesson on how to say let’s and let him or her in Estonian: the we form without me (teeme, vaatame, lähme) and las with the present (las ta tuleb, las nad mängivad, las ma aitan). Every sentence is read aloud."

msgid "A free lesson on saying “I have” in Estonian: mul, sul, tal, meil, teil and neil on, names with -l such as Maril on, the question kellel on?, mul on vaja for I need, and how to answer with jah, on or ei ole. Every sentence is read aloud."
msgstr "A free lesson on saying “I have” in Estonian: mul, sul, tal, meil, teil and neil on, names with -l such as Maril on, the question kellel on?, mul on vaja for I need, and how to answer with jah, on or ei ole. Every sentence is read aloud."

msgid "A free lesson on saying no in the Estonian past: ei and the -nud form (ma ei läinud, nad ei lugenud), the same for every person, built from the -da form, with five to learn by heart, plus ei olnud, polnud and mul ei olnud aega. Every sentence is read aloud."
msgstr "A free lesson on saying no in the Estonian past: ei and the -nud form (ma ei läinud, nad ei lugenud), the same for every person, built from the -da form, with five to learn by heart, plus ei olnud, polnud and mul ei olnud aega. Every sentence is read aloud."

msgid "A free lesson on saying where you are in Estonian: -s for in (Tallinnas, koolis), -l for on and at (laual, tööl), which places take which, and how to answer kus, “where”. Every sentence is read aloud."
msgstr "A free lesson on saying where you are in Estonian: -s for in (Tallinnas, koolis), -l for on and at (laual, tööl), which places take which, and how to answer kus, “where”. Every sentence is read aloud."

msgid "A free lesson on the Estonian -ma, -mas and -mast forms: lähen ujuma (going swimming), olen ujumas (out swimming) and tulen ujumast (back from swimming). They answer kuhu?, kus? and kust? as places do. Also when to say lähen, käin or tulen. Every sentence is read aloud."
msgstr "A free lesson on the Estonian -ma, -mas and -mast forms: lähen ujuma (going swimming), olen ujumas (out swimming) and tulen ujumast (back from swimming). They answer kuhu?, kus? and kust? as places do. Also when to say lähen, käin or tulen. Every sentence is read aloud."

msgid "A free lesson on the Estonian conditional mood, the would form: -ks with the person endings (ma läheksin, sa läheksid, ta läheks), polite requests (ma tahaksin kohvi, kas sa saaksid aidata?) and if clauses (kui mul oleks aega). Every sentence is read aloud."
msgstr "A free lesson on the Estonian conditional mood, the would form: -ks with the person endings (ma läheksin, sa läheksid, ta läheks), polite requests (ma tahaksin kohvi, kas sa saaksid aidata?) and if clauses (kui mul oleks aega). Every sentence is read aloud."

msgid "A free lesson on the Estonian days of the week: the seven names, the four with numbers in them, the one-letter days on opening hours, and täna, homme and eile. Every word is read aloud."
msgstr "A free lesson on the Estonian days of the week: the seven names, the four with numbers in them, the one-letter days on opening hours, and täna, homme and eile. Every word is read aloud."

msgid "A free lesson on the Estonian genitive case: minu, sinu, tema, meie, teie and nende for my, your, his or her, our and their, the owner before the noun (Mari raamat), kelle for whose, and the genitive as the form that takes -s and -l (kooli, koolis). Every sentence is read aloud."
msgstr "A free lesson on the Estonian genitive case: minu, sinu, tema, meie, teie and nende for my, your, his or her, our and their, the owner before the noun (Mari raamat), kelle for whose, and the genitive as the form that takes -s and -l (kooli, koolis). Every sentence is read aloud."

msgid "A free lesson on the Estonian imperative: the verb’s stem as a command (loe!, istu!, tule siia!), ära for don’t, the -ge and -ke forms for more than one person or polite you, everyday commands such as räägi aeglasemalt, palun, and a short conversation at a café. Every sentence is read aloud."
msgstr "A free lesson on the Estonian imperative: the verb’s stem as a command (loe!, istu!, tule siia!), ära for don’t, the -ge and -ke forms for more than one person or polite you, everyday commands such as räägi aeglasemalt, palun, and a short conversation at a café. Every sentence is read aloud."

msgid "A free lesson on the Estonian impersonal voice, for when it doesn’t matter who does it: -takse and -akse now (Eestis räägitakse eesti keelt, tehakse), -ti and -di in the past (maja ehitati), ei with -ta (siin ei suitsetata), and Estonian customs to read. Every sentence is read aloud."
msgstr "A free lesson on the Estonian impersonal voice, for when it doesn’t matter who does it: -takse and -akse now (Eestis räägitakse eesti keelt, tehakse), -ti and -di in the past (maja ehitati), ei with -ta (siin ei suitsetata), and Estonian customs to read. Every sentence is read aloud."

msgid "A free lesson on the Estonian object case: the third form for part of something, an action still going on, after ei and after verbs such as ootama (ma loen raamatut), and the genitive for the whole thing done (ma lugesin raamatu läbi), with the dictionary form in commands (osta pilet!). Every sentence is read aloud."
msgstr "A free lesson on the Estonian object case: the third form for part of something, an action still going on, after ei and after verbs such as ootama (ma loen raamatut), and the genitive for the whole thing done (ma lugesin raamatu läbi), with the dictionary form in commands (osta pilet!). Every sentence is read aloud."

msgid "A free lesson on the Estonian partitive case, a noun’s third form: after numbers from two up (kaks kassi, viis eurot), after mitu and amounts (tass kohvi), after pole (mul pole kassi) and with food and drink (ma joon kohvi). Every sentence is read aloud."
msgstr "A free lesson on the Estonian partitive case, a noun’s third form: after numbers from two up (kaks kassi, viis eurot), after mitu and amounts (tass kohvi), after pole (mul pole kassi) and with food and drink (ma joon kohvi). Every sentence is read aloud."

msgid "A free lesson on the Estonian past tense (lihtminevik): -si- on most verbs (elasin, lugesin, rääkisin), -i- on a few everyday ones (olin, läksin, sõin), the endings that say who did it, and eile and eelmisel nädalal. Every sentence is read aloud."
msgstr "A free lesson on the Estonian past tense (lihtminevik): -si- on most verbs (elasin, lugesin, rääkisin), -i- on a few everyday ones (olin, läksin, sõin), the endings that say who did it, and eile and eelmisel nädalal. Every sentence is read aloud."

msgid "A free lesson on the Estonian perfect and pluperfect: olema with the -nud form (ma olen Tartus käinud, ma olin juba söönud), and when to use them instead of the simple past (eile ma käisin Tartus). Every sentence is read aloud."
msgstr "A free lesson on the Estonian perfect and pluperfect: olema with the -nud form (ma olen Tartus käinud, ma olin juba söönud), and when to use them instead of the simple past (eile ma käisin Tartus). Every sentence is read aloud."

msgid "A free lesson on the Estonian plural cases: build the plural genitive by adding -de or -e to the third form (majade, raamatute, inimeste), then add the endings you already know: majades, inimestele, sõpradega. Every sentence is read aloud."
msgstr "A free lesson on the Estonian plural cases: build the plural genitive by adding -de or -e to the third form (majade, raamatute, inimeste), then add the endings you already know: majades, inimestele, sõpradega. Every sentence is read aloud."

msgid "A free lesson on the Estonian plural partitive, the third form for more than one: palju raamatuid, poes on õunu, its three endings -id, -sid and a changed last vowel (autosid, raamatuid, maju, koole), and how it differs from kaks raamatut and the plural raamatud. Every sentence is read aloud."
msgstr "A free lesson on the Estonian plural partitive, the third form for more than one: palju raamatuid, poes on õunu, its three endings -id, -sid and a changed last vowel (autosid, raamatuid, maju, koole), and how it differs from kaks raamatut and the plural raamatud. Every sentence is read aloud."

msgid "A free lesson on the Estonian plural: the second form plus -d (kassid, raamatud), the stem change it keeps (poed, toad, mehed), the verb with a plural noun (lapsed loevad), and adjectives in the plural (suured majad). Every sentence is read aloud."
msgstr "A free lesson on the Estonian plural: the second form plus -d (kassid, raamatud), the stem change it keeps (poed, toad, mehed), the verb with a plural noun (lapsed loevad), and adjectives in the plural (suured majad). Every sentence is read aloud."

msgid "A free lesson on the future in Estonian, which has no future tense: the present with homme, ülehomme and järgmisel nädalal (homme ma töötan), and hakkama with a -ma form for what you’re going to do (ma hakkan õppima). Every sentence is read aloud."
msgstr "A free lesson on the future in Estonian, which has no future tense: the present with homme, ülehomme and järgmisel nädalal (homme ma töötan), and hakkama with a -ma form for what you’re going to do (ma hakkan õppima). Every sentence is read aloud."

msgid "A free lesson on the three forms of an Estonian verb, lugema, lugeda and loen: how the -da form is made with -da, -ta, -la, -na or -a, including teha, näha, süüa and juua, and the -ge and -ke commands built on it. Every word and sentence is read aloud."
msgstr "A free lesson on the three forms of an Estonian verb, lugema, lugeda and loen: how the -da form is made with -da, -ta, -la, -na or -a, including teha, näha, süüa and juua, and the -ge and -ke commands built on it. Every word and sentence is read aloud."

msgid "A free lesson on which Estonian verbs take the -da form after them and which take the -ma form: tahan lugeda but pean lugema, with võin, saan, oskan, tohin, proovin, armastan and mulle meeldib on one side and hakkan, lähen, tule and jään on the other. Every sentence is read aloud."
msgstr "A free lesson on which Estonian verbs take the -da form after them and which take the -ma form: tahan lugeda but pean lugema, with võin, saan, oskan, tohin, proovin, armastan and mulle meeldib on one side and hakkan, lähen, tule and jään on the other. Every sentence is read aloud."

